head("2.5 Two conditions of the night: urgency and public transport", 2)
para("*Urgency.* We treat urgency as a condition of the night and not as a construct that the phenomenon discloses in its own terms. It is a short, externally fixed window within which a response must be made, because a threatened act will otherwise be completed or the chance to prevent it will pass, with suddenness, a deadline set from outside, and a cost of delay. The window for the Assembly to act was a matter of hours, and by the author’s account the time from the declaration to his arrival was under two hours. We do not claim that urgency is new; work on triggering events and moral shocks addresses events that spark mobilization quickly. What we need from it is its role in setting the clock a population must meet, which separates the 2016–17 protests, announced in advance, from the night of 3–4 December, and which in Gwangju in 1980 met an infrastructure that was largely closed. *Public transport.* The capital is compact and its public transit dense, so that the author’s own trip took about fifty minutes. The mayor reportedly urged staff that night to keep daily life, including transportation, unchanged (“Oh Se-hoon,” 2024), and we found no report that transport was closed to the public, though we found no record of how it ran. The digital layers told people what had happened and where to go, and public transport carried them there. We treat it as a condition that makes going easy for those who decide to go.")
head("2.6 The core question: do minjung subjectivity and infrastructure work together under urgency?", 2)
para("The constructs are unlikely to be independent, and the question that the phenomenon raises most sharply is whether it lies in their conjunction. *Convergence* is the outcome: dispersed people become a co-present gathering at the place where an institution can act, within the time that matters. Minjung subjectivity supplies the recognition of the event, the willingness to treat it as one’s own task, and self-direction. Infrastructure supplies the means of seeing that others are moving, of checking what one is told, of learning where to go, and of keeping those channels open against suppression; public transport lets people get there, and urgency sets the clock. The episode suggests, without testing it, that neither alone produces convergence. Infrastructure without minjung subjectivity may circulate information so that an audience forms and no gathering does; we know of no case in this paper that shows this. Minjung subjectivity without plural infrastructure may remain isolated, as the accounts of Gwangju in May 1980 suggest; the comparison is a candidate contrast and not a test, since 1980 differs in regime, force and institutional route. The 2016–17 protests are not a test of this question, since no emergency required a response within hours. Table 3 sets out the possible patterns.")
table([
 ["", "Many independent channels, no single command", "One command over the channels, or few channels"],
 ["Minjung subjectivity present", "Convergence: recognition, witnessing and verification, routing to the institution. Candidate case: 3–4 December 2024", "Isolation: readiness without the means to see, verify or gather. Candidate case: Gwangju, May 1980"],
 ["Minjung subjectivity weak or absent", "Circulation without convergence: an audience, not a gathering. Conjecture; no case yet", "Little response. Baseline"],
], 8)
cap("Table 3. Possible patterns, under urgency, if minjung subjectivity and infrastructure work together (conjectures), with candidate cases.")

para("*Relation to social movement research.* A conjunction of will and means resembles the synthesis in social movement research of political opportunities, mobilizing structures and framing processes (McAdam et al., 1996). The question here includes whether minjung subjectivity reduces to frame resonance and plural infrastructure to a mobilizing structure. We think not wholly: recognition is suggested to precede any organizational alignment and to be held by a population, and the infrastructure was not designed for mobilization and was under no single organization’s command. If the constructs do reduce to these, the indigenous label adds a name and not a concept, which further evidence can show.")
head("2.7 Rival accounts and indigenous specificity", 2)
para("The episode admits several accounts, and the constructs are useful only if they add to them: (1) institutional procedure and members’ own initiative sufficed; (2) the restraint of security forces explains the outcome; (3) leaders and journalists, not the population, drove events; (4) parties, unions and civic groups supplied direction; (5) a connected population explains the speed, with no need for history or self-understanding; (6) the difference between 1980 and 2024 lies in regime, force and institutional route; (7) the cost of attending was low, since a compact capital with dense public transit made going easy for anyone who learned of the declaration, whereas in our account transport is an enabling condition. The first two challenge enabling presence, the fourth minjung subjectivity, the fifth its recognition face, and the sixth and seventh the conjunction. We do not expect any single account to be sufficient; the aim is to state what the constructs would add and what evidence would show that they add nothing. *Indigenous specificity.* Minjung subjectivity and the minjung event are drawn from minjung theology and are named in its language, because “the public,” “citizens” and “the networked individual” omit a collective subject defined by domination. The other constructs and the two conditions are what they operate under. What is specific to the setting is the conjunction of remembered martial law, an achieved democracy, a state-seeded but privately operated infrastructure, a compact capital with dense public transit, a constitutional route for override and an articulated tradition about the people as subjects of history. We do not claim that the tradition is unique to Korea, since it is related to, though distinct from, liberation theologies elsewhere (Sung, 2018).")
head("3. Implications for IS theory")
head("3.1 Digital collective action and infrastructure", 2)
para("*Digital collective action.* Accounts tend to treat mobilization as a process over weeks; urgency suggests asking what a population’s infrastructure lets it do within hours. Platform-centered accounts lead one to expect that how quickly people joined depended on the structure and reach of networks, whereas the question raised here is whether it depended on how people recognized the event, on how many independent channels reached them, and on the two together. *Digital infrastructure.* The constructs raise the question of who can command which layer in a crisis, and add to the installed-base idea (Star & Ruhleder, 1996) an account of functions, witnessing and verification, that an infrastructure serves for a population without being designed for them.")
head("3.2 Critical IS: minjung, Habermas and the exception", 2)
para("Critical and emancipatory IS research is most often associated with Habermas’s critical social theory (Hirschheim & Klein, 1994; Myers & Klein, 2011). As we read it, critique is grounded in communicative action, in which participants coordinate by raising validity claims that can be redeemed by reasons (Habermas, 1984); legitimacy comes from discursive procedures through which communication from civil society reaches the legislature and law (Habermas, 1996); and civil disobedience is a principled protest that tests a constitutional state (Habermas, 1985). In IS this has meant treating emancipation as the removal of constraints on rational discourse and participation. Minjung theology starts instead from the experience of a dominated people: its subject is the minjung, constituted in the act (Jeong, 2023), and emancipation is the minjung’s own act. We read Habermas through this IS stream and secondary summaries.")
para("Under urgency this model strains, because two of its assumptions were removed on the night. The first is protected communicative space: the decree barred all political activities and placed all media under the Martial Law Command, so the conditions of discourse were themselves the target. The second is time: deliberation presupposes that the constitutional order stands while people argue, whereas the window for the one institution that could end the decree was a matter of hours. In these conditions the crowd’s act was less a contribution to discourse than a defense of the institution through which discourse reaches law, and the work of the infrastructure was less to host an argument than to let people witness, verify and gather. We do not say that Habermas’s theory cannot be extended to such a case; we say that the minjung account starts from it.")
para("The suspension of a constitutional order is also a subject of political theory: Schmitt ties sovereignty to the decision on the exception (Schmitt, 1985), Agamben argues that the exception has become a normal paradigm of government (Agamben, 2005), and work on militant democracy asks how democracies may defend themselves through law against movements that would end them (Loewenstein, 1937a, 1937b). The crowd’s act does not fit the last frame: the actor was a population, the means were presence and information, and the threat came from the holder of emergency powers. The declaration used a logic of the militant kind, justifying exceptional measures as protecting a liberal order from anti-state forces (“South Korea President Yoon Suk Yeol Declares Emergency Martial Law,” 2024), and the Constitutional Court later held it illegal, finding that its actions violated the rule of law and the basic principles of a democratic country (Gong, 2025); we report the Court’s findings and do not judge motive. We therefore classify the crowd’s act as a defense from below of the institutions that decide who threatens democracy; this is our classification and not an established category.")
para("The minjung tradition is not a theory of the exception, and we found no text in it that teaches the defense of institutions as a doctrine; that reading is ours. It did, however, take shape under emergency rule, and two of its founders signed the 1976 declaration that demanded the abolition of the emergency measures and the restoration of parliamentary politics and judicial independence (National Institute of Korean History, n.d.). What it adds to critical IS is a second question, beside how systems can enlarge discourse: how infrastructures help people defend the institutions on which discourse depends when those institutions are threatened within hours. That shifts critique from the design of a system for participation to the conditions under which a population can witness, verify and gather without any one actor commanding the channels, takes the standpoint of the dominated as a source of knowledge, and names a danger, the use of the people’s name to dominate it (Lee, 2007). We call the direction minjung emancipatory IS, not an established theory; it rests here on one case.")
head("3.3 Contribution to theory and next steps", 2)
para("The contribution of context is new concepts and an account of their conditions (Venkatesh, 2025). The constructs arise from the setting, and two are named in its language. They can be developed by extending the author’s account into a set of accounts, by obtaining aggregate carrier and platform logs, by interviews across roles and political views that include people who did not go to the Assembly, and by comparing episodes, before any propositions are stated. The principal risks are a single case, retrospective accounts, political sensitivity, the particularity of minjung theology and the absence of voices that supported the declaration.")
head("Appendix A. Archived streams cited by the author")
para("Stream 1: an opposition party leader of the time, https://youtu.be/9vh9Uimmvqc, a live broadcast beginning at 11:00 p.m. on 3 December 2024. Stream 2: the independent broadcaster Lee Dong-hyung TV, “Emergency Broadcast,” https://youtu.be/T3uQ2uQxKDk, from 11:00 to 11:31 p.m. Times and wording are as reported by the author.", A.LEFT, 1.0, 6, indent=False)
